exec tool declaration:
```ts
declare const tools: { mcp__pixellab__create_image_pro(args: {
  // What to draw (e.g. 'ornate iron key with a ruby inlay').
  description: string;
  // Canvas height. Max depends on aspect ratio.
  height?: number;
  // At most this many colours in each image (2-64), e.g. 6 for an NES-style sprite. With a palette, they are picked from it.
  max_colors?: number | null;
  // Return subjects on a transparent background. Defaults True — pro is normally used for assets. Set False for scenes and backdrops.
  no_background?: boolean;
  // Target palette, 2 to 256 hex colours as a JSON array, e.g. ["#081820", "#346856", "#88c070", "#e0f8d0"] for Game Boy or ["#000000", "#ffffff"] for 1-bit. The image is drawn for these colours and every pixel of the result is one of them.
  palette?: Array<string> | null;
  // Optional name of the palette (e.g. 'Game Boy', 'PICO-8', 'NES'); naming it helps the look.
  palette_name?: string | null;
  // Internal Game Builder project context; the finished image is linked after generation.
  project_id?: string | null;
  // Up to 4 LABELLED reference images, as a JSON array. Each entry takes a "url" (preferred) OR a "base64": [{"url": "https://...", "usage": "character base"}, {"url": "https://...", "usage": "outfit and armour"}]. The "usage" note tells the model what to take from each image, which is what makes combining several references work. Prefer urls here especially — four inline images in one argument is four times the payload, and MCP clients truncate large arguments. Sizes are read from the images.
  reference_images?: Array<{ [key: string]: unknown; }> | null;
  // Seed for reproducible generation.
  seed?: number | null;
  // Which aspects to take from style_image_base64: any of color_palette, outline, detail, shading. Defaults to all four, or all but color_palette when a palette is given.
  style_copy?: Array<string> | null;
  // One image whose art style (palette, outline, detail, shading) the result should match. Use style_copy to narrow which of those apply.
  style_image_base64?: string | null;
  // Alternative to style_image_base64: an https (or data:) URL to the style PNG. PREFER THIS for anything but a tiny sprite. Give one, not both.
  style_image_url?: string | null;
  // Canvas width. The cap is an AREA of roughly a quarter megapixel, shaped by whichever of ten aspect ratios your request is nearest to — so no single number is the maximum width. Those maxima: 1:1 512x512, 5:4 576x464, 4:3 600x448, 3:2 632x424, 16:9 688x384, 21:9 792x336, and the portrait transposes (4:5 464x576, 3:4 448x600, 2:3 424x632, 9:16 384x688). 792x336 is the widest canvas there is; 704x304 is nearest 21:9 and fits. Anything over its ratio's pair is refused before the job is queued.
  width?: number;
}): Promise<CallToolResult>; };
```

Queue a pixel-art UI panel (10-25 generations, ~2-4 min). Returns ui_asset_id immediately.
Omit `pieces` for a default rounded-rect panel. Use get_ui_asset to check status.
For a fixed palette (Game Boy, PICO-8, any palette from Lospec) pass `palette` as
its hex colours: the panel is drawn for them and every pixel is one of them.

Cost: 10 generations for a canvas up to 256x256, 15 up to 341x341, 25 above
— so the 256x256 default is the cheap option and 512x512 is 25. A non-square
panel is measured at its own aspect ratio, which is more generous than its
longer side: a 16:9 panel is 10 up to 341x192, 15 to 456x256, 25 from 457
wide. There is no quote endpoint; the response reports the actual charge.

